      "content": "We administer electroporation-independent Cas9–sgRNA ribonucleoprotein complexes conjugated to methylated polyethylene glycol at 300 µg/ml to induce site-specific double-strand breaks within the HBB locus in primary human CD34+ hematopoietic stem cells under normoxic conditions. Subsequent genome-wide screening utilizes next-generation sequencing (NGS) paired with CRISPR-Engage workflows for spatial mapping of DNA repair intermediates, employing a bioinformatic pipeline that leverages deep learning models trained on DiVERGE and TargetScan databases to calculate percent identity thresholds, Chi-squared goodness-of-fit for off-target indel frequencies, and Bayesian posterior probability distributions to distinguish true variant signatures from background noise. Concurrently, mass spectrometry-based proteomic profiling validates the retention of HAT1/HPF1 kinase activity post-modification, ensuring epigenetic reprogramming fidelity while modeling the kinetic half-life of the truncated RNP complex within the nuclear envelope against reference standards. This multi-omics integration yields high-fidelity single-cell resolution data regarding chromatin remodeling kinetics and potential p53-mediated apoptosis suppression mechanisms specific to the targeted β-globin allele.",
